This appendix presents a formal derivation of the relation between Borel transforms within the same resurgence class; equation \eqref{eq:finding-Borel-rep-from-other} in the main text. Let us consider a family of asymptotic series in the same resurgence class---with the class specified by the constants $\alpha$ and $ eta$ (different sets yielding different classes) and the family $\{ \Phi_{[\gamma]} \}$ parameterized by $\gamma$---which is given by  egin{equation} \Phi_{[\gamma]} (\lambda) = \lambda^\gamma\, \Phi_{[0]} (\lambda) \simeq \sum_{k=0}^{+\infty} \Phi_k\, \lambda^{\alpha k +  eta + \gamma}, \end{equation} 
oindent and where we are taking $\Phi_{[0]}$ to be the standard representative. This essentially means that the coefficients $\Phi_k$ have the precise (large-order) factorial growth of  egin{equation} \Phi_k \sim \left( \alpha k +  eta - 1 ight)!, \end{equation} 
oindent and that the Borel transform ${\cal B} [\Phi_{[0]}] (s)$ will have the singular structure \eqref{simpleBorelsingularities}. All other Borel transforms, associated with each other element in this family of functions, will be defined as  egin{equation} {\cal B} [ \widehat{\Phi}_{[\gamma]} ] (s) = \sum_{k=n_0(\gamma)}^{+\infty} \Phi_k\,  rac{s^{\alpha k +  eta + \gamma - 1}}{\Gamma \left( \alpha k +  eta + \gamma ight)}. \end{equation} 
oindent Let us explain the notation. In order to obtain the Borel transform of the generic element $\Phi_{[\gamma]} (\lambda)$, one first needs to remove the first $k<n_{0}(\gamma)$ terms within its asymptotic expansion, in such a way that $\alpha\, n_{0}(\gamma)+ eta+\gamma>0$ and the Borel transform is thus well-defined. This ``truncated'' asymptotic series is then denoted by $\widehat{\Phi}_{[\gamma]} (\lambda)$. As to the terms which get removed, they are handled separately, and can be added back-in at the very end, once we have Borel resummed and obtained ${\cal S}_	heta \widehat{\Phi}_{[\gamma]}$ following the standard procedures. In order to simplify our present discussion, we will not consider these terms as they are always straightforward to add at any stage. For integer values of $\gamma$, the straightforward relation (with $n,m \in {\mathbb N}^+$)  egin{equation}  rac{s^n}{\Gamma \left( n+1 ight)} =  rac{{\mathrm{d}}^m}{{\mathrm{d}} s^m}\,  rac{s^{n+m}}{\Gamma \left( n+m+1 ight)} \end{equation} 
oindent directly implies the relation between Borel transforms ootnote{As an historical aside, note that this sort of relations was probably first noticed in \cite{bpv78b}, but, unfortunately, the role of the standard representative---with its (simple) logarithmic ramified structure, and where resurgent analysis becomes most straightforward---was not emphasized enough to spark subsequent further analysis.}  egin{equation} {\cal B} [ \widehat{\Phi}_{[\gamma']} ] (s) = D_s^{\gamma-\gamma'} {\cal B} [ \widehat{\Phi}_{[\gamma]} ] (s). \end{equation} 
oindent Assuming that $\gamma-\gamma'$ is an integer and that $\gamma>\gamma'$, the operator $D_s^n$ is herein defined as the $n$th derivative with respect to $s$. The relation \eqref{eq:relation-between-borels-der} also holds if $\gamma<\gamma'$, in which case one simply defines $D_s^n$ as the $n$th 	extit{primitive} ootnote{Each primitive yields an integration constant, and upon multiple primitivation one obtains a polynomial in $s$ with maximum degree $\gamma'-\gamma$. This polynomial is directly related to the first $n_0(\gamma)$ coefficients which were removed from the original asymptotic series $\Phi_{[\gamma]} (\lambda)$ in order to obtain the ``Borel transformable'' asymptotic series $\widehat{\Phi}_{[\gamma]} (\lambda)$.} with respect to $s$. If $\gamma=\gamma'$ then $D_s^0$ is simply the identity. The assumption that $\gamma-\gamma'$ is an integer may be lifted, allowing \eqref{eq:relation-between-borels-der} to be valid for a 	extit{continuous} parameter $\gamma \in {\mathbb R}$. Let us show how this is done when ootnote{The case where $\gamma<\gamma'$ follows in complete analogy by simply inverting \eqref{eq:relation-between-borels-der} and having the operator $D_s^{\gamma'-\gamma}$, which is now a 	extit{derivative}, acting on ${\cal B} [ \widehat{\Phi}_{[\gamma']} ] (s)$.} $\gamma>\gamma'$. In this case we have to deal with a 	extit{fractional derivative}, where $D_s^\alpha$ is defined, when $0<\alpha<1$, as (see, 	extit{e.g.}, \cite{lot76})  egin{equation} D_s^\alpha f(s) =  rac{1}{\Gamma \left(1-\alphaight)}\,  rac{{\mathrm{d}}}{{\mathrm{d}} s} \int_0^s {\mathrm{d}} t\,  rac{f(t)}{\left( s-t ight)^{\alpha}}. \end{equation} 
